410: isomorphism $\phi$ on $M^{\times,\circ}$. That is, the generator of $\bZ$
411: acts by the lift of $t_{6P}^{-1}$ defined by $\phi$.
412: \end{definition}
413: 
414: So in particular,
415: the automorphism of $M^{\times,\circ}$ induced by $\phi$ lifts
416: the automorphism $t_{6P}^{-1}$ of $S^\circ$.
417: 
418: \begin{proposition}\label{discontinuous}
419:  Fix a reference linearization $\phi$ as in Corollary
420: \ref{lin-exist}. There exists a constant
421: $c>0$ such that for all linearizations $u\phi$, $u\in \bC^*$, with $|u|<c$, the $\bZ$-action 
422: (Definition \ref{z-action}) associated to $u\phi$ is free and properly discontinuous. \end{proposition}
423: 
424: \begin{proof}
425: Consider the map
426: $
427: \phi\colon t_{6P}^*M\to  M
428: $ which is an isomorphism away from
429: $S_\infty$.
430: Choose a smooth Hermitian metric on $M$. With respect to this metric,
431: the operator norm
432: $
433: x\mapsto \|\phi_x\|
434: $
435: is a continuous function on the compact surface $S$, equal to zero 
436: exactly along $S_\infty$. It is therefore bounded. Set
437: $
438: C=\max_{x\in S}\|\phi_x\|
439: $
440: and choose $c>0$ such that $cC<1$.
441: 
442: Fix $u\in\bC^*$ with $|u|<c$. Then,
443: $u\phi$ also defines a self-map of $M$
444: covering $(t_{6P})^{-1}$ as in Definition~\ref{z-action}. 
445: Since the operator norm of $u\phi_x$ is strictly
446: less than $1$, the action is contracting 
447: with respect to the Hermitian norm function $M^{\times,\circ} \to \bR_{>0}.$
448: The $\bZ$-action on this open set is therefore free and properly discontinuous.
449: \end{proof}
450: 
451: \begin{definition}\label{linearization-def}
452: Let $\phi_0$ be a linearization as in Corollary \ref{lin-exist} and let
453: $\phi = u\phi_0$ with $|u|<c$, $u\in \bC^*$ as in Proposition \ref{discontinuous}.
454: We define a smooth threefold as the quotient 
455: $$X^\circ(u)\coloneqq M^{\times,\circ}/\bZ$$
456: of the $\bZ$-action of Definition \ref{z-action} associated 
457: to the linearization $\phi$.
458: \end{definition}
459: 
460: \begin{proposition} $X^\circ(u)\to \bA^1$ is a fibration with
461: smooth complex $2$-torus fibers over $\bA^1\setminus \{0,1\}$.
462:  \end{proposition}
463:  
464:  \begin{proof} Over $t\in \bA^1\setminus \{0,1\}$ we have an exact sequence
465:  \begin{align}\label{exact} 1\to \bC^*\to M^\times_t \to S_t\to 0,\end{align}
466:  i.e.~the fiber of $M^\times$ over $t$ is a semiabelian variety.
467:  So the universal cover of $M_t^\times$
468:  is $\bC^2$, and its fundamental group is isomorphic to $\bZ^3$.
469:  
470:  Quotienting by the additional
471:  $\bZ$-action associated to the linearization $\phi$ gives a complex $2$-torus,
472:  because the action is properly discontinuous, see Proposition \ref{discontinuous},
473:  and the additional $\bZ$-factor increases the rank of the subgroup of $\bC^2$ from $3$ to $4$.
474:  \end{proof}
475:  
476: \begin{proposition}\label{mumford-1}
477: In a punctured neighborhood of $\infty\in \bP^1$ with
478: coordinate $q$,
479: $X^\circ(u)_q$ is biholomorphic to $$(\bC^*)^2/\langle (c_1q, c_2), 
480: (c_3, c_4q) \rangle$$ for holomorphic units $c_i\in \bC^*+O(q)$ on $\Delta$.
481: \end{proposition}
482: 
483: \begin{proof}
484: Since $S_\infty$ is an $I_1$ Kodaira fiber, the restriction of $S$ to
485: $\Delta^*$ admits a Tate uniformization
486: $
487: S_q\simeq\bC^*/q^\bZ.
488: $
489: After pulling $M^\times$ back to the partial cover
490: $\bC^*\to S_q$ and choosing a trivialization, its total space is
491: $(\bC^*)^2$. The deck transformation defining the Tate curve acts by
492: $$
493: (z,w)\mapsto
494: (c_1q z,c_2w), \textrm{ for }(z,w)\in (\bC^*)^2.
495: $$
496: Here $c_1$ and $c_2$ are holomorphic units. The first scaling factor has
497: order one in $q$ because it is the Tate period, while the second has
498: order zero because $M$ has degree zero and extends across the $I_1$
499: fiber.
500: 
501: The linearization $\phi$ gives a second transformation of
502: $(\bC^*)^2$. It covers translation by $-6P$ on the Tate curve. Since
503: $6P$ specializes to a point of the smooth locus of the $I_1$ fiber,
504: its multiplicative Tate coordinate is a holomorphic unit. Thus the
505: first component of this transformation has the form $z\mapsto c_3z$.
506: Moreover, $\phi$ vanishes to first order along $S_\infty$ so its
507: action in the fiber direction of $M^\times$ has the form
508: $w\mapsto c_4 q w$, where $c_4$ is a holomorphic unit. Hence the second
509: transformation is
510: $$
511: (z,w)\mapsto
512: (c_3z,c_4 qw), \textrm{ for }(z,w)\in (\bC^*)^2.
513: $$
514: 
515: Quotienting $(\bC^*)^2$ by these two commuting transformations gives
516: the asserted description of $X^\circ(u)_q$ for $q\in \Delta^*$.\end{proof}
517: 
518: \begin{proposition}\label{mum-cor}
519: A $\bZ^2$-periodic tiling 
520: of $\bR^2$ by lattice simplices of area $\tfrac{1}{2}$ determines
521: a Kulikov ($K$-trivial, semistable)
522: filling $$X^\circ(u)\hookrightarrow X(u)$$
523: over $\infty\in \bP^1$.
524: For example, one may take the tiling by triangles in Figure \ref{kulikov-fig}.
525: In this case, the fiber
526: $X(u)_\infty$ is the result of gluing the opposite sides of a hexagon of $(-1)$-curves
527: on a del Pezzo surface $dP_6$.\end{proposition}
528: 
529: \begin{proof} This follows from a simple Mumford fan construction
530: \cite{Mum72}. By Proposition \ref{mumford-1}, the
531: fiber over the punctured disk is $(\bC^*)^2$ modulo the subgroup
532: generated by the rows of the matrix
533: $$
534: c\cdot q^{\begin{psmallmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{psmallmatrix}},
535: \qquad
536: c={\small \begin{pmatrix}c_1&c_2\\c_3&c_4\end{pmatrix}} ,
537: $$
538: where $\cdot$ denotes entrywise multiplication.
539: Thus, a $\bZ(1,0)\oplus \bZ(0,1)\simeq \bZ^2$-periodic
540: tiling of $\bR^2$ provides a Mumford construction filling the family over $q=0$.
541: Take the triangulation
542: of the unit square into two triangles. Extending
543: $\bZ^2$-periodically gives such a tiling
544:  $\cT$. Put $\cT$ at height $1$ in $\bR^3$, i.e.~$\bR^2\times \{1\}\subset \bR^2\times \bR\simeq \bR^3$,
545:  and take the cone ${\rm Cone}(\cT)$. The result is a $\bZ^2$-periodic fan in $\bR^3$
546:  with standard affine cones. 
547:  
548:  This fan defines a one-parameter Mumford construction of degenerating
549:  complex $2$-tori, as in  \cite[Constr.~3.3 and Rem.~3.5]{EGFS25}:~we take the $\bZ^2$-quotient 
550:  of the toric variety of ${\rm Cone}(\cT)$, for which the fiber $(\bC^*)^2$ over $q\in \Delta^*$ of the natural map to $\bA^1$ is
551:  quotiented by the $\bZ^2$-action of Proposition \ref{mumford-1}. 
552:  
553: Before quotienting, the central fiber is an infinite periodic quilt of smooth toric surfaces, whose components
554: are isomorphic to $dP_6$ (see Figure \ref{kulikov-fig}). The passage to the $\bZ^2$-quotient has the effect
555:  of taking a single such $dP_6$ and gluing the opposite sides
556:  of its anticanonical hexagon, as described in the proposition.\end{proof}
557:  
558:  \begin{figure}
559: \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{kulikov-fig.png}
560: \caption{Left:~$\bZ^2$-periodic tiling $\cT$ of the plane $\bR^2$. 
561: Middle:~$\bZ^2$-periodic infinite quilt of smooth toric surfaces.
562: Right:~Its $\bZ^2$-quotient and the central fiber of Mumford construction, a glued
563: $dP_6$. Two $0$-dimensional
564: torus orbits in white and grey, three $1$-dimensional torus orbits in black; the $2$-dimensional
565: torus orbit in blue.}
566: \label{kulikov-fig}
567: \end{figure}
568: 
569: 
570: \begin{proposition}\label{section-of-J}
571: The fibration $X(u)\to\bP^1$ admits a holomorphic section.  Over
572: $\bP^1\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}$, this section may be taken as the origin of
573: the smooth complex $2$-torus fibers.
574: \end{proposition}
575: 
576: \begin{proof}
577: The restriction of $M$ to the zero section $O$ admits a section $e$
578: which is non-vanishing over $\bA^1$ and has a first order zero at $\infty$,
579: because $M\cdot O=1$. Hence 
580: $e$ defines a section of $M\vert_O\to O$, valued
581: in $M^\times\vert_O$ away from $\infty$. Its image under
582: the quotient map of Definition \ref{linearization-def} defines
583: a section of $X^\circ(u) =M^{\times,\circ}/\bZ \to \bA^1$.
584: 
585: Near $\infty$, the condition that $e$ vanishes to first order
586: implies that it defines a local section of the
587: uniformization of Proposition~\ref{mumford-1}. Indeed, we may first
588: translate $e$ by the period $(c_3^{-1}, c_4^{-1}q^{-1})$. 
589: This translated section has nonzero limit at $q=0$, and so extends 
590: as a section of the Mumford construction. It coincides with 
591: $e$ in the quotient $X^\circ(u)$.
592: \end{proof}
593: 
594: We have thus completed the summary of the ``initial construction'': We have produced
595: a smooth, compact complex threefold $X(u)$ for all sufficiently small $u\in \bC^*$,
